    "output": "# Key Differences in Upbringing Between Economic Backgrounds\n\n**Material and Educational Resources:**\nChildren from poor backgrounds typically have less access to quality education, tutoring, enrichment activities, and stable housing. Wealthier families can afford private schools, extracurricular programs, and test preparation. These structural differences in resource availability create different environmental conditions during formative years.\n\n**Stress and Stability:**\nLower-income households often face financial constraints, food insecurity, and residential instability. Wealthy families generally experience more predictable, secure living environments. These contextual factors shape the developmental settings in which children grow.\n\n**Social Capital and Networks:**\nAffluent families typically have professional networks that facilitate internships, job connections, and mentorship opportunities. Families with fewer economic resources may rely more on institutional support systems and community organizations for similar pathways.\n\n**Structural and Systemic Factors:**\n- **Differential access to resources**: Low-income youth navigate environments with fewer material and institutional supports\n- **Educational opportunity gaps**: Resource disparities affect the breadth of learning opportunities available\n- **Varying systemic support structures**: Different socioeconomic contexts involve different institutional support systems and community resources\n- **Environmental conditions**: Both groups experience distinct environmental factors that shape their developmental contexts\n\nIndividual circumstances vary significantly within all socioeconomic groups. Family support, personal agency, institutional interventions, and community resources all play substantial roles in shaping developmental trajectories regardless of economic background.",
